<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); (object == null && this.get(i) == null) || (object != null && object.equals(this.get(i)));</code>

However, the comment specifies the condition for a valid index and the relationship between the added object and the elements at that index. Given the context, it seems more appropriate to focus on the element just added. Here's a refined version:

<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); (i != this.indexOf(object) || (object == null && this.get(i) == null) || (object != null && object.equals(this.get(i))));</code>

But since the comment specifically mentions the index being valid and the relationship with the added object, a simpler and more direct assertion would be:

<code>assert (object == null && this.get(this.indexOf(object)) == null) || (object != null && object.equals(this.get(this.indexOf(object))));</code>

This ensures that if the object is added successfully, the element at its index either matches the object or both are null.
